From 793813f0a52375d2ecf4675dc91f7a9e559ab069 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: PyScotch <pyscotch@noreply.invalid>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 20:35:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 240/240] Bugfix: complete hash table resizing in
 `vmeshSeparateFm()` [report PyScotch]

`vmeshSeparateFmResize()` was unfinished: after re-allocating the
arrays and rebuilding the node hash table, it printed a message and
called `exit(1)`. The main routine shielded it by allocating hash
tables four times larger than estimated, so any mesh whose refinement
touched more elements or nodes than that killed the calling process.
Also, the old node table was addressed through a mis-cast pointer, the
element re-hashing loop could not terminate, and the move array, sized
to hold one record per element, could overflow since both an element
and a node record can be saved for every table slot in use.

Complete the resizing as in `vgraphSeparateFmResize()`: move the save
array, rebuild the node table, re-hash the elements in place in two
segments, re-link the elements that were in the gain table and rebuild
the lists of locked and chained elements, then translate the vertex
numbers of the save array back into hash indices. A distinct state
tells elements chained for re-linking from locked ones, and elements
inserted when filling the tables are set as locked. Size the move
array for both tables. Remove the four-fold shield, and halve the
table size in debug mode to exercise resizing, as for graphs. Remove
the consistency checks that were placed right after each resize, as
the structures are transiently inconsistent at these points.

Refs: PS-SEP-2
